<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karnataka</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karnataka is a state of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bengaluru</a> and a mainly Kannada-speaking population. It was the heartland of dynasties including the Kadambas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-ganga-dynasty">Western Gangas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a>, Rashtrakutas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoysala-empire">Hoysalas</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, and later of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>. The state was formed as Mysore State in 1956 by uniting the Kannada-speaking areas and was renamed Karnataka in 1973.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnataka.jpg" alt="Karnataka" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apadegal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hampi_virupaksha_temple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur annexed Bidar in 1619, and in the 1630s and 1640s its generals, among them the Maratha commander Shahaji Bhonsle, conquered much of the southern <b>Karnataka</b> plateau and parts of the Tamil country, taking Bangalore in 1638 and the fortress of Gingee in 1649.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas of Badami rose in the mid-sixth century in the valley of the Malaprabha River in northern <b>Karnataka</b>, a region previously dominated by the Kadamba dynasty of Banavasi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hoysalas were hill chiefs of the Malnad, the forested uplands of the Western Ghats in southern <b>Karnataka</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Mysore grew from a small chiefdom in the southern <b>Karnataka</b> plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Dantidurga died c. 756 without a son and was succeeded by his uncle Krishna I, who completed the conquest of the remaining Chalukya lands, subdued the Western Ganga dynasty of southern <b>Karnataka</b> and attacked the Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The colossal statue of Bahubali at Shravanabelagola in <b>Karnataka</b>, carved from a single block of granite around 983, stands about 17 metres high and is anointed with milk, saffron and flowers every twelve years.</blockquote><a href="/religion/jainism">Jainism · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
